Situation
When I joined CBA’s REX modernization project, we had to migrate 200+ Tableau dashboards and complex Pega workflows to a microservices-based cloud architecture.

Two junior associate engineers were assigned to the project:

One had experience in frontend development but limited backend knowledge.

The other had experience in backend development but minimal exposure to frontend technologies.

However, the project demanded full-stack capabilities, and the knowledge gap led to:
❌ Slow feature development, as tasks required constant handoffs.
❌ Code inconsistencies, since each engineer lacked expertise in half of the stack.
❌ Low confidence, as they felt overwhelmed by unfamiliar technologies.

Task
I needed to:
✔ Mentor both engineers to become full-stack developers, equipping them with skills across frontend, backend, and cloud infrastructure.
✔ Improve development efficiency, enabling them to handle features end-to-end.
✔ Foster a collaborative learning environment, where they could share knowledge and support each other.

Action
Following CBA’s leadership principles, I designed a structured coaching plan:

1️⃣ Hands-On Learning & Pair Programming (Be Curious and Humble)

Backend-focused engineer: Assigned React UI tasks to build hands-on experience.

Frontend-focused engineer: Assigned Spring Boot microservices to strengthen backend skills.

Pair programming sessions ensured knowledge-sharing and confidence-building.

2️⃣ Guided Project Work with Gradual Complexity

Initially assigned small independent tasks to build confidence.

Progressed to full-stack user stories, where each engineer handled both frontend and backend components.

Encouraged code reviews between them, reinforcing peer learning and feedback culture.

3️⃣ Knowledge Sharing & Collaboration (Create Exceptional Teams)

Introduced weekly tech-sharing sessions, where each engineer taught the other their area of expertise.

Created documentation and reusable code templates, reducing onboarding time for new engineers.

Encouraged them to lead discussions in sprint reviews, strengthening communication and stakeholder engagement.

Result
🚀 Both engineers transitioned into full-stack roles, becoming self-sufficient in handling end-to-end features.
🚀 Development velocity improved by 35%, as they no longer relied on constant handoffs.
🚀 Their code quality improved significantly, as they gained a deeper understanding of the entire stack.

